# Death Valley landscape build plan

**Status:** Active Blender-first implementation, 2026-10-05. `DeathValleyExploreExpanded.blend` contains full regional tiles and a selective Badwater terrain detail corridor; see the [expansion build record](./studies/DeathValley/EXPANDED_VIEWER.md). The separate [four-slice Badwater scene](./studies/DeathValley/BADWATER_GAME_SLICE.md) now tests native 1 m elevation across 4.096 × 4.096 km, 256 m chunks and provisional close materials. Rocks, player traversal and Unity integration remain open; see the implementation record below.

## Outcome and stop condition

Build a reusable Death Valley-inspired geographic base for the active Unity TopDown3D game. The user's scope is **as much of Death Valley as feasible, ideally the whole park**. The first regional study covered the park bounding rectangle and margin at **176.2 × 215.4 km** in EPSG:26911. Its expanded Blender viewer now covers **192 × 224 km** in full 32 km source tiles, with a selective Badwater detail corridor. Earlier Mosaic Canyon 10 m and 1 m patches remain technique studies. These are separate levels of detail, not a single full-resolution world mesh. The distant Blender terrain is a visual study; any navigationally significant horizon in Unity must come from reachable or intentionally obstructed world plans.

The implementation cycle is done when (1) the geographic data can be rebuilt from a documented source manifest, (2) the Blender study reads as valley, fan, wash, bedrock, and mountain at game and ground-level views, (3) its rock kit has measured geometry and texture costs, (4) a versioned rule and asset handoff explains how the sample constrains other generated places, and (5) one representative Unity chunked proof slice passes the defined visual, traversal, deterministic, streaming, persistence, and performance gates. Red Rock Canyon, Valley of Fire, and the Grand Canyon remain future regional extensions.

## Authority and boundaries

- **Creative authority:** The user requested the broad Death Valley coverage. The Mosaic site is a technical detail study; final palette, geology simplification, visual acceptance, and what becomes game canon stay with the user.
- **World rules:** [WORLD_BASIS.md](../../WORLD_BASIS.md) controls the active 3D game's barren ecology, ancient dry channels, perpetual twilight, and the Broken World's tone. Do not quietly introduce active rivers, vegetation, or ordinary Earth daylight into the game version.
- **Technical authority:** [World Creator charter](../../Design/DeferredGeneration/WORLD_CREATOR_CHARTER.md), [architecture](../../Design/DeferredGeneration/WORLD_CREATOR_ARCHITECTURE_PLAN.md), and [world systems standard](../../Engineering/WORLD_SYSTEMS_STANDARD.md) control causal geography, sample-to-grammar authoring, traversal affordances, deterministic identity, chunk streaming, near/middle/far representations, and persisted deltas. Real terrain is reference data and optional bounded cache, not a finite replacement for the effectively infinite world. The regional Blender study does not replace the approved Fractured Transect proof envelope.
- **Blender lane:** [Blender research](./research/DEATH_VALLEY_TERRAIN_PIPELINE.md) supplies data and technique choices. The existing [badlands study](./studies/HANDOFF_2026-10-01.md) remains paused and dirty; create a separate Death Valley study. Source art and Blender tools are owned here; approved production art goes under `Assets/_Project/Art/`.
- **Unity lane:** Babineaux handles import, scene/prefab/editor work and Unity proof under Gottspan's integration order. Test this geology in a disposable, non-canon proof influence. The charter's first playable territory has rolling hills and no canyon system or deep canyon cuts; this study does not silently change it. No normal repository task should focus the user's Unity window.
- **Exclusions:** Google Maps/Earth-derived mesh or extracted textures, one giant full-resolution mesh, purchased tools as prerequisites, Unity gameplay smoke tests, and unrelated Unity/Blender cleanup.

## Sequence and acceptance gates

| Stage | Build work and concrete output | Gate to continue |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **0. Site, interface and source preflight** | Use the NPS park boundary, USGS elevation, NAIP reference imagery, and geologic map. Check 1 m DEM availability, rights, local GIS libraries, and current Unity world-query/catalog interfaces. Define raw-data storage and reproducible acquisition. Record the target Unity proof machine, camera, baseline, and provisional budgets before integration. | Full park coverage is bounded; sources and rights are documented; the Unity proof contract has one owner and measurable configuration. |
| **1. Aligned GIS grids** | Produce a float 200 m region, 10 m Mosaic tile, and 1 m detail patch, imagery references, categorical geology, slope/ruggedness, and wash/fan/cliff/talus placement masks on aligned metric grids. Keep raw data outside Unity imports; record hashes, CRS, datum note, NoData, transform and dimensions. | Re-running produces matching grids; tile edges share source samples, NoData is explicit, geologic categories do not interpolate, and sample elevations match source values within tolerance. |
| **2. Blender terrain study** | Create `build_region_study.py` for the installed Blender version, reading the prepared manifest. Write a *new* local-origin `.blend` with context and hero terrain collections, source metadata, fixed review cameras, and an untouched DEM base plus authored terrain deltas. Build one representative tile first, then the bounded hero area. | Terrain scale, orientation and silhouettes match source hillshade; no visible tile cracks; identical game-view and ground-view renders can be compared across iterations. Record evaluated vertices/triangles, RAM and render time on this machine. |
| **3. Terrain forms and surface** | Add limited authored cliff/ledge meshes where the heightfield cannot represent vertical shape. Prototype macro palette from imagery, regional blends from geology/landforms, and tileable micro PBR material maps. Preserve a barren Broken World interpretation instead of directly draping Earth imagery. | At least one valley-floor-to-rock transition looks coherent from the elevated game camera and close view; material families and cliff silhouettes remain readable under a twilight lighting test. Geometry is reserved for silhouette/collision-relevant relief. |
| **4. Rock kit and debris** | Build a small kit: planar stratified slabs, fractured cliff pieces, angular dark boulders, talus, and low-cost pebble variants. Make one high/low baked hero example; use reusable tiling maps where unique bakes add little. Build one Geometry Nodes scatter group driven by geology, slope, wash/fan/cliff masks, exclusions, family, seed and density. | Fixed-view comparisons show broad planar faces rather than clay-like lumps. Record per-mesh evaluated triangles, UV/normal checks, texture sizes, LODs and representative scatter counts; review contact, burial and repetition. |
| **5. Extract the reusable grammar** | Document the sample's causal relationships: strata and rock-family selection; basin/ridge/fan/wash shapes; cliff-to-talus and wind/deposition rules; sparse versus dense spaces; horizon hierarchy; route and overlook intent. Export approved meshes/maps and masks only where the Stage 0 interface calls for them. Label any exact-height cache as bounded reference data. | The rule handoff specifies how a second composition can vary silhouette, route and spacing without copying the complete 2 km arrangement; Stage 6 tests that claim in generated output. Rule/catalog versions and ownership are explicit. |
| **6. Unity proof slice** | Babineaux imports a *bounded, disposable proof influence* through the existing Unity World Creator path, without changing first-playable geography. Preserve source/export separation and paired `.meta` files. Check the existing [landscape benchmark](../../Design/DeferredGeneration/TOP_DOWN_3D_LANDSCAPE_BENCHMARK.md) and World Creator proof contract against current code before setting thresholds. | Import scale/pivot/orientation/materials pass. Fixed-camera panels show near/mid/far agreement, multiple approaches, purposeful empty space and no obvious kit repetition. Seed and absolute coordinates reproduce placement; neighboring chunks and route/affordance queries agree; Booter and BigARM have the intended access; unloaded-world route truth remains valid; unload/reload restores stable IDs and saved deltas. A Development Player profile reports chunk integration, frame time, renderer/collider counts and texture memory against the Stage 0 budgets. User-owned hands-on traversal and visual acceptance remain a separate gate. |
| **7. Expansion decision** | Compare results with the user's visual target and measured costs. Choose whether to expand Death Valley, add another geologic province, deepen the rock kit, or trial a paid terrain tool for a specific unmet need. | Expand only after the first slice demonstrates clear visual gain and manageable runtime/data cost. Record any changed geographic scale or art rule before rebuilding. |

Stages 0–2 formed the first **data-to-Blender vertical slice**. The current review stays on regional terrain geometry and geographic color coverage in the overview-only `.blend`; the multi-resolution master and rock/scatter prototype are preserved for later passes. Stage 6 is a separate Unity integration batch; the Blender study alone is not gameplay proof.

## Minimal pipeline contracts

The GIS output manifest should include: source IDs/URLs and hashes, license, acquisition date, geographic/projected CRS, vertical datum, projected bounds, local Blender origin, grid width/height/resolution, NoData, resampling method, mask legend, and generator version. Height must remain in metres; any artistic vertical scale or displacement must be an explicit versioned layer. All tiles sample a shared grid and border convention.

The Blender exporter should emit: named mesh family and LOD, dimensions in metres, contact pivot, evaluated triangle count, UV/material map list, export transform, source `.blend` identity, mask grid identity, and catalog version. Blender Geometry Nodes are a **placement preview**; do not export millions of realized rocks or assume Unity can consume Blender's shader/Geometry Nodes graph.

The Unity adapter should bind the grammar, catalog and any needed masks to **seed + independent generator versions + precision-safe absolute location**, with larger-scale authored geology, routes and landmarks constraining placement. Stage 0 defines whether each input is an authored rule, a bounded geographic sample/cache, or a disposable proof fixture; Stage 5 evaluates that choice before export. The owning chunk may instantiate and unload visuals but cannot invent its own geology. Feature IDs must remain stable across those transitions. Store player/simulation changes as deltas rather than saving generated mesh instances. Blender's UTM origin is source-data metadata, not the game's future thematic coordinate system.

## Budget and risk controls

- **Size:** The expanded 192 × 224 km 200 m source grid is 2.15 million triangles before its detail cutouts. The layered Blender viewer is 4.75 million visible terrain triangles. A whole-park 10 m mesh would be excessive. Keep the region coarse, tile nearby areas, and stream game representations by distance. Treat resolutions as initial measurements, not permanent budgets.
- **Data fidelity:** 10 m elevation cannot provide boulder, crack, undercut, or traversable ledge detail. Use authored feature meshes, surface maps, and instanced rocks; check 1 m coverage only for selected detail sites.
- **Visual fidelity:** Aerial photos can contain shadows and color bias. Use them for palette and masks, with artist correction and close-range PBR textures. Real Death Valley geology is reference, not a demand for literal replication.
- **Runtime:** Stage 0 records the proof machine, quality setting, fixed production camera, relevant baseline and provisional budgets; Stage 6 checks them in a Development Player. Material count, texture memory, collision, instance density and build time matter alongside source triangles. No final performance claim follows from Blender timings or Unity source inspection.
- **Dependency cost:** Blender + QGIS/GDAL + USGS + CC0 is the default. A World Machine or Adobe purchase requires a specific demonstrated gap, license review, and user decision. BlenderGIS is optional only after version compatibility is verified.
- **Preservation:** Keep large raw geodata and generated caches out of incidental Unity imports and Git until storage and provenance policy is chosen. Do not alter the paused badlands `.blend` or other dirty Unity files to execute this plan.

## Implementation record, 2026-10-05

- **Regional coverage:** NPS boundary query for `DEVA` returned EPSG:26911 extent `409313,3938827,565540,4134109`; the prepared study uses `[399400,3928800,575600,4144200]`. The overview grid is 882 × 1078 vertices at 200 m. It is a geographic study, not Unity runtime geometry.
- **Expanded viewer:** The current full-tile grid is `[399400,3920200,591400,4144200]`, 961 × 1121 vertices at 200 m. It adds 15.8 km east and 8.6 km south with source data. A candidate 48 × 40 km Badwater corridor has 40 m terrain, an 8 × 8 km 20 m basin-edge pilot, and a 2 × 2 km 10 m eastern canyon patch. See the [verified build record](./studies/DeathValley/EXPANDED_VIEWER.md); exact corridor visual acceptance is pending.
- **Close studies:** Mosaic tile `[486500,4045800,488500,4047800]` is 201 × 201 vertices at 10 m. The 1 m patch `[486650,4046900,487150,4047400]` is 501 × 501 vertices. The official USGS 1 m source covers this patch.
- **Prepared data:** [`tools/death_valley/`](./tools/death_valley/) builds and verifies the GIS grids. The raw 1 m tile and geological archive live outside Git in `/Users/worldbuilder/Desktop/Death Valley Terrain Data/raw/`. The external manifest records service responses and hashes. The geology map covers 67.5% of the broader regional grid and 100% of Mosaic; code `0` explicitly means unknown, never an interpolated rock unit. NAIP imagery covers 88.8% of the broad reference image.
- **Current Blender viewer:** `DeathValleyExplore.blend` contains the 200 m regional grid as 42 mesh objects and 1,897,674 triangles. It opens in a free User Perspective view over Racetrack Playa; Home frames the whole region. All tiles are loaded together, with no game camera or streaming needed at this resolution. Natural-color Landsat imagery provides the regional surface reference. Individual Racetrack stones and tracks are below this grid's detail level.
- **Preserved detail study:** `DeathValleyTerrainMaster.blend` contains the same regional terrain, about 83,000 visible Mosaic triangles with a stitched transition to the regional grid, and a 500,000-triangle 1 m patch. Mosaic uses higher-resolution NAIP plus a CC0 scanned rocky-ground normal/roughness layer. The earlier separate rock/scatter scene remains available but is deferred. Its five rock meshes have 38–76 triangles each, with 1,630 Geometry Nodes preview instances; twenty 512 px procedural rock maps are starter art only.
- **Current visual limit:** The regional silhouette and source scale are established; the near view still reads as a rough blockout. The flat rock forms, sparse clutter variation, and simple procedural surface need an art pass before production acceptance. Full geologic coverage and Unity proof remain open.
- **Stage status:** 0–2 implemented for the Blender study; the full-tile regional expansion and selective Badwater terrain detail are built. The four adjoining Badwater game-scale slices now have 256 aligned 256 m chunks, four 1 m focus tiles, exact source-matched terrain edges, provisional material masks and candidate height exports. The saved scene and preview renders passed checks; interactive review on the user's Mac remains pending. Stages 3–4 are prototype only; 5 handoff is drafted; 6 Unity proof waits for Blender review. No claim of gameplay, streaming, persistence, collision, or Development Player performance proof follows from these scenes.
